  "policy_title": "Extend UK Shared Prosperity Fund",
  "outcome": "O14",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O14",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "For three years the policy continues a fund aimed at disadvantaged areas, which modestly supports regional equality. But after that, the money is redirected to a National Service scheme distributed by the number of 18-year-olds, which would shift funding away from poorer regions toward wealthier ones \u2014 widening regional inequality.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the National Service scheme is ever implemented as described, or whether a future Spending Review substitutes a different funding model before the pivot happens.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy extends the UKSPF for three years before redirecting its funding to a National Service scheme focused on life chances and skills.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "extend the UK Shared Prosperity Fund for three years at the next Spending Review, before using this funding to support UK-wide National Service, focusing on increasing life chances, civic pride, and skills"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The UKSPF is specifically targeted at economically disadvantaged areas as the main formula-based levelling-up fund.",
        "evidence_id": "E17",
        "quote": "The UKSPF is described as the \"main formula-based and flexible source of levelling up funding targeted at economically disadvantaged areas.\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The UKSPF runs at approximately \u00a31.5 billion per year by 2024-25.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "funding reaching \u00a31.5 billion per year by 2024-25"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "From 2028-29, the \u00a31.5 billion per year previously allocated to the UKSPF would be diverted to fund the National Service scheme.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "the \u00a31.5 billion per year previously allocated to the UKSPF would be diverted to help fund a new UK-wide National Service scheme"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The National Service scheme would distribute funding based on the number of 18-year-olds rather than economic disadvantage, creating a significant disconnect with levelling-up goals.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "The National Service scheme, however, would likely distribute funding based on the number of 18-year-olds in an area, creating a \"significant disconnect\" between levelling up rhetoric and actual funding policy, as it would be less targeted at disadvantaged areas"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "This reallocation would leave poorer regions substantially worse off \u2014 for example Wales ~\u00a3275m/year worse off and the North East and Tees Valley ~\u00a346m/year worse off \u2014 while some South of England areas gain.",
        "evidence_id": "E18",
        "quote": "Wales would be approximately \u00a3275 million per year worse off, Cornwall \u00a372 million worse off, and the North East and Tees Valley \u00a346 million worse off"
      },
      {
